                    "Lecture II — Fear is not the opposite of courage",
                    """
                    Fear belongs to human emotional life. It perceives possible harm and prepares the person to protect what matters. Fear of losing work may reveal responsibilities to dependents. Fear before a medical result acknowledges bodily vulnerability. Fear in a threatening relationship may carry urgent safety information. A person without any fear could be ignorant, numb, impaired, reckless, or simply unaware. Feeling afraid is not itself a moral failure, and an involuntary bodily response does not prove cowardice.

                    Fear can also distort. Anxiety may treat a remote possibility as certain, shame may exaggerate social rejection, memory may carry an old danger into a safer present, and manipulation may teach someone to fear lawful help. Fortitude does not silence these experiences through insult. Prudence asks what danger is real, how serious and probable it is, whom the choice affects, and what protection or counsel is fitting. Clinical anxiety, trauma, panic, or obsessive fear may require professional care; receiving that care can be part of courageous truth rather than an admission of weak faith.

                    Cowardice is more precise than fear. It occurs when a person abandons a good that should be chosen because fear has been permitted to rule against sound judgment. Even then, culpability cannot be inferred by an app from outward behavior. Knowledge, freedom, trauma, coercion, capacity, and circumstance matter. Formation should name a missed duty without condemning the whole person. The practical task is to make fear speak accurately, place it beside the good and available supports, and recover the next free action.
