                    Rashness often borrows noble language. A person may call an impulsive confrontation “speaking truth,” unsafe rescue “self-sacrifice,” refusal of medical care “trust,” or ignoring procedure “putting people first.” The stated good can be real while the means exposes others to preventable harm. Ask who is responsible, what competence is needed, whether delay increases danger, which safeguards are available, and what less harmful means can serve the same end. Preparation does not dilute courage. It directs strength toward success without treating persons as proof of boldness.

                    Avoidance also disguises itself. Endless preparation, waiting for total certainty, transferring every difficult conversation, or using peace as a name for silence may leave another person unprotected. Prudence distinguishes genuine missing information from the wish that discomfort disappear. A useful test is whether delay has a reason, owner, action, and review time. “I will verify the record and report tomorrow through the safeguarding lead” is prudent timing. “I will act when I feel braver” may simply give fear indefinite authority.
