                    Motives are often mixed. A worker may report fraud from concern for customers and resentment toward a supervisor. A parent may set a necessary boundary while also wanting to punish. A believer may defend truth while enjoying public praise. Mixed motives do not always require paralysis until the heart becomes pure. They require honesty, repentance, and structures that let the good govern. Remove humiliating language, use accountable channels, seek counsel from someone who will not flatter anger, and accept an outcome in which the truth is served without personal victory.

                    Good intention also cannot transform an evil means. Saint John Paul II points to martyrdom as witness that some acts must be refused even under extreme pressure. A lie, abuse, deliberate attack on the innocent, or other intrinsically evil act does not become courageous because it protects reputation, livelihood, group loyalty, or even life. Fortitude gives the will strength to remain faithful to moral truth when a shortcut promises relief. This is demanding, but it safeguards the dignity of every person, including the actor.
