                    First Corinthians 13 tests impressive action. Speech, knowledge, faith, generosity, and even bodily surrender can fail to become love. Charity is patient and kind; it is not boastful, self-seeking, irritable, or rejoicing in wrongdoing. A public act may be dramatic yet spiritually empty if it uses people as scenery. A quiet refusal, apology, or protection may be deeply courageous because love remains central when applause is absent.

                    Love also governs treatment of opponents. Courage can name evil precisely, resist it firmly, and use legitimate authority without denying the wrongdoer's humanity. Meekness is not weakness. It restrains the urge to answer humiliation with humiliation and keeps anger available for protection and correction. Gaudete et Exsultate warns against verbal violence and connects inner strength with meekness. When a difficult action leaves room for truth, due process, conversion, and proportionate protection, strength has begun to serve charity.
