                    Fortitude has an active face that advances against obstacles and an enduring face that stands firm when difficulty cannot be removed quickly. Classical Christian reflection often treats endurance as the deeper test because an intense action can be supported by adrenaline, while long fidelity must survive ordinary fatigue. Caregiving, recovery, study, prayer, marriage, honest work, grief, chronic illness, and reform may require hundreds of choices that no audience sees.

                    Endurance is active. It continues to choose the good available today, protects hope, adjusts means, receives support, and refuses both despair and retaliation. Resignation says nothing can be done and may abandon the sphere of freedom that remains. Endurance can grieve what cannot be changed while still asking, “What does love require now?” This question may lead to rest, treatment, complaint, a boundary, a smaller task, or patient presence rather than simply doing more.

                    Joseph Rickaby's emphasis on endurance is useful when governed by contemporary pastoral safeguards. Suffering is not automatically sanctifying, and preventable harm should not be prolonged to prove virtue. Abuse, medical danger, coercion, unsafe work, and treatable illness call for protection and help. One can endure the difficult process of leaving, reporting, healing, or rebuilding rather than endure the injustice itself. Fortitude bears the cost of the good; it does not make harm into the good.
